WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.680
- He's a rock legend known

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.280
around very impressive halls

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.710
such as the Rock &amp; Roll Hall of Fame,

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:07.240
the Songwriters Hall of Fame,

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.490
and true to his roots,

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.310
the New Jersey Hall of Fame.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:11.143
There you go Anthony.

00:00:11.143 --> 00:00:11.976
That's for you.

00:00:11.976 --> 00:00:15.730
He's sold over 130
million records worldwide.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.150
His new album is called "2020",

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:20.720
and it is much, much better

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.270
than the actual year it's
named after, obviously,

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.649
because it's a low bar, right? (laughs)

00:00:25.649 --> 00:00:26.482
(audience chuckles)

00:00:26.482 --> 00:00:27.840
It's a bad year.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.239
Give it up for Jon Bon Jovi, y'all.

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:31.672
(upbeat music)

00:00:31.672 --> 00:00:34.589
(audience applaud)

00:00:36.372 --> 00:00:37.205
Hello!

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:39.853
- Hey.

00:00:39.853 --> 00:00:40.686
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:40.686 --> 00:00:43.610
I'm first of all, so excited
that you're on my show.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:44.930
I can't believe my life.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:46.823
Okay, so be honest.

00:00:46.823 --> 00:00:48.370
And when I say be honest,

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.360
I mean lying is totally okay.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.020
How did I do with your song? (laughs)

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:53.853
Did you hear it?

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:55.020
- It was awesome.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.870
I noticed that you had to change the key.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:58.380
It was in a girl key.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.160
- I know I'm a first soprano man.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.030
I always have to raise everything

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:02.960
because I can't go low.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.680
I can go high. (laughs)

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:05.940
- Yeah. (chuckles)

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:07.380
God bless you for it.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.600
But yeah, it was cool.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:09.880
Thank you for doing that.

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:10.940
- Oh my gosh, no.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:12.430
We're all such big fans.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:13.930
So where were you in life

00:01:13.930 --> 00:01:15.150
when you wrote "It's my life,"

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.310
and what does it mean to you?

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.210
- For me, it was Frankie.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:20.180
Like there's a lyric

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.520
and it says like, "Frankie
said I did it my way."

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.990
That was from new Jersey's
most famous son, Frank Sinatra.

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.410
And I had just come home

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:32.370
from doing a film in Malta
and Rome called "U-571."

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.350
It was a world war II picture
with Matthew McConaughey

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:39.334
and we'd come home and we're
making the Crush record

00:01:39.334 --> 00:01:41.900
and we got together to write that

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.750
and I had to fight very
hard for that lyric.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:47.357
And Richie said to me, "What is Frankie?

00:01:47.357 --> 00:01:48.720
"And why does it matter?"

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.767
And I says, "Well, because much like Frank

00:01:50.767 --> 00:01:52.247
"I'm going to make movies when I want,

00:01:52.247 --> 00:01:53.667
"records when I want,

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:55.060
"and this is how I'm feeling."

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:57.570
And I won the argument
simply because I had

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:58.940
to sing the song every night.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.800
And that's what it stood for it for me.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.310
But the magic was is guys

00:02:03.310 --> 00:02:05.530
like Anthony and people around the world,

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:07.057
they said, "No Frankie is me.

00:02:07.057 --> 00:02:08.357
"Frankie's my best friend.

00:02:08.357 --> 00:02:09.980
"Frankie's my brother."

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:10.813
And that's the magic

00:02:10.813 --> 00:02:13.230
of a great song is
people make it their own.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.620
- Yeah the message yeah that you can get.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:16.590
That's the most interesting thing

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.380
I think performing shows
and meeting fans afterwards

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.700
and everything everybody
has their own kind

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.080
of take on a song that you had
a completely different idea

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:23.990
about why you wrote it.

00:02:23.990 --> 00:02:24.823
And I think that's

00:02:24.823 --> 00:02:26.380
what's so magical about music.

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:27.490
But what what's one

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:31.300
of your songs you do not look
forward to singing on tour?

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.730
'Cause I have a couple
of my own. (chuckles)

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.700
- Well, it's not that I don't look forward

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.940
to singing any one of them.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:40.390
Some of 'em are easier than others.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.870
Some of 'em are, you know,

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.630
selfishly the one that you want to do.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.290
But you know I mean,

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:50.100
there's some that I wrote
or co-wrote depending

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.250
on which song it was

00:02:51.250 --> 00:02:53.870
that are ridiculously high

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:56.280
and youth speaks to that.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.154
You know, when you're 25, it's one thing.

00:02:59.154 --> 00:03:01.150
(laughs) And you're like, ah.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:02.427
- I mean I just did one and I'm like...

00:03:02.427 --> 00:03:04.787
Literally the thing I
said after I sang it was,

00:03:04.787 --> 00:03:07.640
"How does he do this
song like every night?"

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.086
Like all these songs and that rasp.

00:03:10.086 --> 00:03:13.420
I would sound like Janis
Joplin in like 36 hours.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:14.522
It wouldn't last long.

00:03:14.522 --> 00:03:15.355
(man laughs)

00:03:15.355 --> 00:03:17.780
But like I said, your
catalog is incredible.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.000
So it's an honor to get
to even sing one of 'em.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.990
What do you call rock music?

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:24.070
- It's hard to really define.

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:27.550
I mean, rock music was
influenced by the blues

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:30.180
and rockabilly and some of
the lyrics were country.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.730
So it became its own thing from the blues.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:37.790
But I guess it's an
energy and it's a spirit.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.150
That's probably at the core of it all.

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:41.390
- I always find this interesting.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:42.740
Somebody with a catalog like yours,

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:44.750
like what was the first song you wrote?

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.082
And is it horrible?

00:03:46.082 --> 00:03:47.530
(all laugh)

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:48.868
- Sure it was horrible.

00:03:48.868 --> 00:03:49.701
Of course it was, yeah.

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:53.390
Every little kid who was strums a guitar.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:54.880
Yeah, but that's the idea, isn't it?

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.090
You're supposed to emulate those who came

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.040
before you and then you
eventually find your own voice

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.950
and eventually you want to play it

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:06.210
for somebody whether it's
your friends or your family.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:08.980
But like I've been telling
a lot of folks recently

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:11.930
write a song and I don't
care if you make a living out

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.090
of this write it for you because it's

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:16.840
the closest thing you'll
ever know to immortality.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.320
Even if you write it in your bedroom

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:19.940
and it never gets published,

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:20.773
that's not the point.

00:04:20.773 --> 00:04:22.500
The point is is you created it

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:23.963
and it'll be yours forever.

00:04:24.802 --> 00:04:26.180
- Ah, that's so beautiful.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:27.570
I've never thought of it like that.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:29.180
The immortality of it.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.690
Is it true that your first industry job,

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:34.170
'cause we all had tons of
jobs before this obviously,

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.350
but were you a runner
in a recording studio?

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:38.530
- Amongst other things yeah.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:40.930
But by that time, which
was very, very early on

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:43.280
I had just graduated high school.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.930
But by that point I was
only playing original music.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:50.030
And so it was difficult to
keep an original band together.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:53.440
And so I had an opportunity
through a distant cousin

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.530
to work in a recording
studio that he owned.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:57.610
And so I was the gopher.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:58.660
Yeah.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:05:01.120
- That's actually awesome though
to have an in at a studio.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.830
God, I can't imagine like

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.470
I mean my first job was in a movie theater

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.303
and I was a waitress.

00:05:05.303 --> 00:05:06.136
Like I would've loved

00:05:06.136 --> 00:05:08.000
like one of my first jobs
to be able to do that.

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:09.170
- I worked in a shoe store,

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:12.450
in a fast food joint you
know, an auto body shop,

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.700
and sold newspaper
subscriptions door to door.

00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:18.320
You were just asking a music business job.

00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:20.470
But yeah, yeah I had odd jobs.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:22.640
I was just, you know, like yourself.

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:26.010
I got a record deal
when I was 21 years old

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:28.460
and I've been with the
same label ever since.

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:29.293
- Oh wow.

00:05:29.293 --> 00:05:31.010
- So, you know, I was fortunate enough

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:32.760
to have been very young.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:33.960
- Let me talk about your album though

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.310
because it's interesting
you called it "2020".

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.890
So did writing about the
year help you through it?

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:41.840
'Cause this has been a rough year.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:44.180
Mind you I hope it's
progressive at some point.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:45.920
I hope we progress and we learn from it

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.930
and we don't just sit in it.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:50.810
But I mean, is that kind of
as an artist I know we write

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:51.770
about stuff that we're going through,

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.910
is that kind of why you ended up writing?

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:56.600
- Early on when we recorded this record

00:05:57.560 --> 00:05:59.140
the title meant something
completely different.

00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:01.090
It was about clear vision

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.380
and then as the songs
started to take shape

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.990
and they became more and more topical

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:11.830
I thought, okay, I am
just witness to history.

00:06:11.830 --> 00:06:15.080
And if I could pretend
to be just the narrator,

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.630
just the voyeur.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:17.620
Don't take sides.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:20.220
Even on the pointed songs,

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:22.030
I could start a conversation.

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:23.820
And so it is a very topical song.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.650
It addresses COVID, it
addresses gun violence,

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:30.510
soldiers with PTSD, Colin Kaepernick,

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:31.840
the current administration.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.580
I mean, it's really a lot that happened

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:37.870
to all of us in the last year,

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:39.730
but I don't think that it's a down record.

00:06:39.730 --> 00:06:42.010
I think it's a rather upbeat record

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:46.590
and songs like "Do What You
Can," are very optimistic.

00:06:46.590 --> 00:06:48.130
- I think it's the greatest song

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:50.420
in the world when it's
a conversation starter.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:52.870
Like as a writer, I feel like
if it starts a conversation

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.900
I'm like, "Oh, I did
something good there."

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:55.990
Like, you know, it, wasn't just

00:06:55.990 --> 00:06:57.220
a selfish thing to get it out of me

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:58.053
or be the vessel for.

00:06:58.053 --> 00:07:01.100
But there was one song
on the album that came

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.930
about because of this picture.

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:04.000
What's the story there?

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:06.120
'Cause that I love you for this.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:08.200
Like you're so rad. (laughs)

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:10.380
I love it you're just in
the back doing dishes.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.670
- Well that is one of
the JBJ soul kitchens.

00:07:12.670 --> 00:07:14.660
We have three of them.

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:16.570
We're a community restaurant

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:20.110
where an inmate population
and a paying customer come

00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.210
and that's what make our world go around.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.180
But the basis of it is volunteerism.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:26.150
And because of COVID,

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:27.933
we couldn't have any volunteers

00:07:27.933 --> 00:07:31.080
in that position for
example washing dishes.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:32.650
Dorothea took that photograph.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.050
I wanted to post it on the social media,

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:36.670
looking for the day of the week and

00:07:36.670 --> 00:07:39.040
the time of the day that we would be open

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.963
to feed that in-need population.

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:43.950
What I responded with was

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.200
if you can't do what you
do, you do what you can.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.310
The next day when I woke up,

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:50.620
I went, "Well, there's a big
old Bon Jovi song title."

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:51.810
So I wrote it

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:54.630
and it's about the COVID crisis,

00:07:54.630 --> 00:07:56.410
but it really addresses

00:07:56.410 --> 00:07:58.810
that all of us can be that difference

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:00.190
that we want to see.

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:02.960
And if you can't do what you do, IE sing,

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.160
do what you can, which
was to make a difference

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.210
in somebody's life that
day by serving them food.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:09.430
- Man, it seems so simple,

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:10.958
but if we all thought that way

00:08:10.958 --> 00:08:11.791
(audience claps)

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:13.980
we'd be a lot further
than we are right now.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.730
So with your JBJ Soul Foundation

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:18.480
you started a food bank as well, right?

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:19.360
So how's that going?

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:20.414
- Yeah.

00:08:20.414 --> 00:08:22.460
So like I said, we have
the three restaurants

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:24.560
and we were feeding
that in-need population

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:26.760
but in the place where
we spent our summers

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:29.390
we realized that the need was really dire.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:32.610
And so we opened a food bank

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:34.130
and we were the supplier

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:37.250
of seven food pantries with their foods

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:39.900
and Dorothea and I conceptualized that.

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:41.270
And within three weeks

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.820
we had gotten the food, found this space,

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:46.460
and we're providing the
food to seven pantries.

00:08:46.460 --> 00:08:49.250
So I think we fed around 25,000 people

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:50.760
over the course of the summer

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:52.200
on Eastern long Island.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:53.033
- Wow.

00:08:53.033 --> 00:08:55.260
Well what a way to
actually make a difference

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.560
and not just sit around your house.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:58.490
I mean, goals right there. (chuckles)

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:01.130
Well it is so lovely to see you.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:02.380
I'm such a big fan.

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:03.670
We all here at the show

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:05.560
and everybody here is freaking out.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.440
I even see this woman with
her hand on her chest.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:10.630
And so Jon Bon Jovi's his
new album is called "2020".

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:11.553
Go buy it.

